gm-method (a file: symlink dep is dev-machine-only and breaks on upload; publish via the dep repo's own CI, do not npm-publish by hand): when an app pins a dependency as `file:../sibling` (npm materializes it as a symlink in node_modules), it works locally but breaks on any upload/deploy/fresh-checkout because the sibling path does not exist there. The fix is to make it a real registry dep. Before assuming `npm install dep@latest` works, CHECK PARITY: the published latest may be an OLD major (npm view dep version + exports) while the local sibling carries an unpublished newer rewrite on a NON-master branch -- the app's imports (incl. importmap subpath specifiers like dep/model-pool, dep/draco-loader) must all resolve to files present in the PUBLISHED tarball's `files` glob and `exports` map, not just on disk. If the dep repo publishes via CI on push-to-master (npm version patch + npm publish), the publish path is: merge the feature branch into master in the DEP repo (resolve the package.json version conflict to the intended version), push, let CI publish; npm version patch bumps from the package.json version so a 2.0.0 package.json -> 2.0.1. Then in the APP repo swap file: -> the published semver pin, rm the node_modules symlink, npm install (lockfile `link` must become null/registry url, not a symlink), restart the static server (its file cache), and browser-witness the app still loads. Add any subpath the app imports (e.g. ./draco-loader) to the dep's exports map while merging. The dep repo is a sibling git repo with its own gh/CI -- operate on it with raw git in its cwd (the gm git verbs are scoped to the app cwd).